  "content": {
    "id": "p11-q7-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "7",
    "prompt": "Solve for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi]$ given that\n$$\\sin\\theta+\\sin2\\theta+\\sin3\\theta=0.$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\theta=0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3},\\pi,\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3},\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2},2\\pi$.\n\nUse sum-to-product to obtain $\\sin2\\theta(1+2\\cos\\theta)=0$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\sin\\theta+\\sin2\\theta+\\sin3\\theta&=\\sin2\\theta+2\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\theta+3\\theta}{2}\\right)\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\theta-3\\theta}{2}\\right)\\\\\\sin\\theta+\\sin2\\theta+\\sin3\\theta&=\\sin2\\theta+2\\sin2\\theta\\cos\\theta\\\\\\sin\\theta+\\sin2\\theta+\\sin3\\theta&=\\sin2\\theta(1+2\\cos\\theta).\\end{align*}$$\nBy the null-factor law, $\\sin2\\theta=0$ or $\\cos\\theta=-\\tfrac12$.\n\nFor $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi]$, we have $2\\theta\\in[0,4\\pi]$.\n$$\\begin{align*}2\\theta&=0,\\pi,2\\pi,3\\pi,4\\pi\\\\\\theta&=0,\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\pi,\\frac{3\\pi}{2},2\\pi.\\end{align*}$$\nThe second factor gives $\\theta=\\tfrac{2\\pi}{3},\\tfrac{4\\pi}{3}$.\n\nTherefore, $\\theta=0,\\tfrac{\\pi}{2},\\tfrac{2\\pi}{3},\\pi,\\tfrac{4\\pi}{3},\\tfrac{3\\pi}{2},2\\pi$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 60
  },
